When I designed the first version of Frame, I gave most of the window to a file list. It made sense for the tool I was building: a desktop interface for FFmpeg, where I could choose conversion settings without remembering command-line flags. Add files, configure the output, start the batch.

Trimming and rotation made the limits of that layout harder to ignore. Those edits needed a visual reference, while the screen was still organized around filenames and parameters. I had made the settings visible before giving the thing they affected a proper place in the interface.

I design and build Frame myself, so these problems followed me from the layout into the implementation. The challenge was keeping the control that made the app useful while making it easier to understand what would happen to a file. Video and audio still needed detailed settings. Batch conversion still needed a queue. The preview had to fit into that workflow.

The two screenshots below show 0.1.0 and 0.34.0 with the same three files loaded. The design decisions between them changed what the screen gives attention to, how settings are grouped, and where editing happens.

## Giving the image enough space

![Frame 0.1.0 on macOS, with a large file queue on the left and conversion settings above an estimated output panel on the right.](/images/blog/frame-design/frame-0.1.0.webp)

_0.1.0. Three files occupy the largest panel, while the settings are compressed into the right-hand column._

The first version has a clear structure, but its proportions don't match the work. The queue is the largest object on screen, even when it contains only a few files. The settings are dense. Storage totals, uppercase labels, and an estimated-output card all ask to be read. There is plenty of empty space, yet no image to help judge an edit.

I wanted the selected file to determine the main view. The queue could remain visible as a way to move between files and manage the batch, with the preview taking the space above it. Keeping the inspector on the right preserved a stable place for settings.

This costs space in the queue. Fewer files fit without scrolling, which matters when working through a large batch. I kept selection and conversion status in the rows so that the smaller panel still works as a queue. Opening a file for inspection and checking it for conversion remain separate actions.

The resulting layout lets me inspect one file without losing the batch around it. The image, its settings, and its place in the queue are visible together.

![Frame 0.34.0 on macOS, showing a large video preview above a smaller file queue, editing tools beside the image, and three labeled inspector tabs.](/images/blog/frame-design/frame-0.34.0.webp)

_0.34.0. The selected media gets the largest surface. The queue remains below it, and the inspector stays alongside it._

## Making the inspector easier to read

Adding settings had gradually stretched the original tab structure. Video, audio, images, subtitles, filters, and metadata each needed controls. At one point, I used icons for the tabs to fit more categories into the available width.

That solved a space problem and created a recognition problem. An icon can be compact without making its meaning obvious. Hover labels helped, but finding a category still depended on knowing its symbol or pausing over it. I was using less room to say something that took more effort to understand.

The current inspector has three named tabs: Metadata, Output, and Presets. They separate reading the source information, deciding what to export, and reusing a configuration. A source frame rate belongs in Metadata; choosing a different frame rate belongs in Output. Metadata editing also lives under Output because it changes the exported file.

Within Output, related settings sit in collapsible sections. This adds an interaction when a section is closed, but it lets the panel show a manageable set of controls at a time. The detailed codec and format choices are still available. They no longer all need a place in the top-level navigation.

I also changed how choices occupy space. Short mode switches can show their alternatives together. Long lists, such as encoders, use select menus. A new option can then fit into an existing control without forcing another redesign of the whole panel.

## Editing where the result is visible

A crop rectangle is easier to understand on the image than through four numbers in a sidebar. Rotation and flipping also benefit from an immediate visual response. I moved those tools beside the preview so that the control and its effect stay close together.

Direct manipulation still needs precision. Dragging a crop is useful for finding a composition; entering dimensions or a ratio is useful when the output has a specific requirement. Frame keeps both. Auto Crop follows the same approach: it proposes a rectangle from detected black bars, leaves it available for adjustment, and waits for Apply. Cancel preserves the previous settings.

That distinction between trying an edit and committing it matters. I want to be able to explore a crop without wondering whether I've already changed the export. The visible selection and calculated output dimensions help explain what applying it will do.

Playback and trim controls now sit over the preview too. Opening the detailed trim panel doesn't shrink the image underneath it. That keeps the visual reference stable while I adjust the range.

Floating controls have a cost: they cover some of the media, and controls revealed on interaction need to be discoverable. Their placement and focus behavior are part of the design. Playback controls can be revealed through hover or keyboard focus, tooltips explain the editing icons, and crop and overlay modes have their own controls. I kept the preview navigation separate from export settings, so zooming in to inspect a detail doesn't change the output dimensions.

The preview also has to be credible. If the displayed crop or subtitle placement disagrees with the export, the interface asks someone to make a decision using misleading information. The [move to an FFmpeg-backed preview](/blog/frame-rewriting-a-tauri-app-in-gpui-ce) was partly an engineering decision, but it supports this design requirement: the picture should be useful for judging the result.

## Changing the visual weight of the controls

The early monospaced type, uppercase labels, and blue selected states gave Frame a technical character. I liked that character, but the repeated treatment made very different pieces of information look similar. A section heading, a format name, and a button label could all have much the same emphasis.

Inter and sentence case give the current interface more variation in the shape of the text. Labels read as words, and hierarchy can come from size, weight, and spacing. In the Metadata panel, the muted field names and brighter values make it easier to separate the label from the information I'm looking for.

The neutral palette serves the same purpose. Video already brings its own color into the window. The surrounding panels use restrained grays, with enough separation to show their boundaries. The active inspector tab is a raised surface inside a recessed rail, so selection doesn't depend on a bright accent color everywhere.

I kept depth because it gives the controls a physical distinction I like. Buttons look raised, fields look inset, and ordinary rows and separators stay flatter. The Graphite treatment draws on Sebastiano Guerriero's surface work, while the control proportions and spacing build on shadcn/ui's Mira style. Nucleo icons share a consistent visual weight across the interface.

The details need to agree. A toolbar with a 14-pixel corner radius and 4 pixels of padding contains controls with 10-pixel corners. That relationship makes the nested shapes sit together. Applying the same spacing and surface rules to menus, dialogs, and settings avoids each part of the app feeling separately styled.

I also removed appearance choices that made that consistency harder to maintain, including the font selector and adjustable window tint. Light and dark themes remain, along with interface scaling from 80% to 200%. Scaling the controls is independent of zooming the preview, so making text comfortable to read doesn't change how the media is framed.

## The behavior behind the appearance

A compact control can still need a generous pointer target. Crop handles have a larger interactive area than their visible marks, and their light fill and dark outline help keep them visible over different images. Sliders and scrollbars received the same attention to the area someone actually has to hit.

Keyboard use exposed another weakness in the early design. I had removed focus rings and suppressed Tab in an attempt to make the interface feel native. That made the appearance more uniform at the expense of navigation. The current controls show focus, the queue supports keyboard selection, and closing dialogs returns focus to a useful place. System reduced-motion preferences also affect interface transitions and preview navigation.

Feedback needed to be more specific as well. A disabled Start button can tell you that something is missing without telling you how to fix it. Frame now explains the blocked state and opens the folder chooser when an output directory is the missing step. That small interaction does more to help someone finish a conversion than another round of button styling.

Looking at the two versions, the change I'm happiest with is the relationship between the picture and the controls. I can keep the frame in view while I crop it, inspect its properties, or decide how to export it. The batch remains close by. Frame still has plenty of technical options, but the layout gives me a more useful place to work through them.
